    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are going to use \"FOILing\", or the method of expanding products of binomials (first on the left, other on the right, inner product on the left, and last on the right), to solve the equation. Then, we will use the basic, well known order of operations:  \n\n$(23)^2 + (2)(23)(2) + (2)^2 = (23)(23) + 2(23)(2) + (2)(2)(2)$\n\nSince we know that multiplication is commutative (e.g. for $a,b,c,d$, $\\; (a)(b)(c)(d) = (a)(b)(c)(d)$), we can group everything four ways until we recognize combinations of an area objective:\n\n$(23)(23) + (2)(2)(23) + (23)(2)$\n\nSince multiplication is commutative, we can swap the terms on the numbers next to 2 and 23. Then, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n$(23)(23) + (23)(2) + ((2)(2))$\n\nNotice the last group $((2)(2))$ can become $(2)(2)(2)$. By assigning a common multiplier, the equation can be rewritten again to get:\n\n$(23)(23 + 2 + 4)$, which is simply $(23)(23 + 6)$\n\nNow that we have all three numbers, we can readily solve through multiplication: \n\n(23)(23 + 6) = 23^2 + 6(23)\n\nThe given example can also be written in polynomial form:\n\n$(23 + 2)^2$ which is equal to $25(23) + 4(23)^2$.\n\nPutting the final answer in the boxed format:\n\n$\\boxed{25(23) + 4(23)^2}$",
